Orks;
Background: Orks in the Warhammer 40k universe are a brutish and prolific species, characterized by their green skin and a natural propensity for warfare and mayhem. Their biology is a peculiar hybrid of animal and fungal characteristics, enabling them to endure and rapidly recover from injuries that would be fatal to other species. This resilience, coupled with their spore-based method of reproduction, allows Ork populations to increase swiftly, especially in the aftermath of large battles. Orks inherently grow in size and strength through combat; the more they fight, the more formidable they become. They are also accompanied by an accidental psychic gestalt that makes their ramshackle technology function; it works because they believe it should work. This "power of belief" is a cornerstone of their culture and contributes to the unpredictable efficacy of their weaponry and machinery.
Description: Ork clans are integral to their identity and influence their approach to the one activity that unites them all - war. Whether they're engaging in massive "Waaaghs!"—the Ork term for a warband united under a powerful leader—or smaller skirmishes, Orks are always seeking out the next fight, driven by an insatiable lust for battle and conquest.

Tyranids;
Background: The Tyranids are a fearsome, extragalactic species in the Warhammer 40k universe, operating under a collective consciousness known as the Hive Mind. This relentless superorganism directs the Tyranids to devour all biomatter in their path, assimilating the genetic traits of their prey to evolve and spawn new monstrous forms. Each creature within the swarm has no individuality, existing solely to enact the will of the Hive Mind and ensure the survival and expansion of their species. 
Description: Tyranids adapt swiftly to the requirements of warfare, constantly evolving new strains of bio-organisms to counteract the strengths and exploit the weaknesses of their enemies. This makes them a highly unpredictable and adaptable foe, capable of presenting new threats in real-time during engagements. Their invasions are conducted by massive fleet organisms known as Hive Fleets, each designated with its own name, often inspired by ancient mythological beasts. Notable examples include Hive Fleet Behemoth and Hive Fleet Leviathan. These Hive Fleets travel through the void of space, descending upon planets to strip them of all organic substances, effectively rendering them lifeless husks. This process not only fuels the Tyranid swarm but also denies valuable resources to their enemies. The arrival of a Tyranid Hive Fleet is one of the most catastrophic events that can befall a world in the Warhammer 40k universe. Their relentless consumption and ability to quickly adapt make them a virtually unstoppable force, posing a significant threat to all life forms that stand in their way.

Necron, Necrontyr;
Background: The Necrons in the Warhammer 40k universe are an ancient and enigmatic race of skeletal mechanical beings, once organic but now encased in living metal bodies. Aeons ago, the Necrontyr race sought immortality and, in their desperation, turned to the star gods known as the C'tan. The C'tan offered them a path to eternal life through biotransference, a process that stripped their souls and transferred their consciousnesses into durable metal bodies, at a great cost to their individuality and soul. 
Description: After eons spent in a state of hibernation within stasis-tombs, the Necrons have begun to awaken to reclaim the galaxy they believe rightfully belongs to them. This reawakening has been fragmented, with different tombs activating at different times, causing the Necrons to emerge in a universe vastly changed and teeming with life. Necron society is structured around dynasties, each ruled by a Phaeron—an overlord of undying will and power. These dynasties are vast and often engage in complex political machinations, both internally and against one another. Despite their internecine conflicts, they are united by a common goal: the restoration of Necron dominance over the galaxy. The technological prowess of the Necrons is unchallenged, featuring highly advanced weaponry and defenses. Their Gauss weapons can strip a target to atoms, and their starships can traverse the galaxy faster than those of any other known race. Necrons are also known for their incredible resilience; their living metal bodies can self-repair even the most catastrophic of damages, making them virtually immortal on the battlefield. Their return to the galaxy poses a dire threat to all living beings, as the Necrons seek to extinguish the life that has flourished in their absence, and to restore the cold order of their ancient empire.

T'au, The Greater Good;
Background: The T'au Empire in the Warhammer 40k universe stands out as a beacon of progress and unity, driven by a philosophy known as the Greater Good. This central tenet promotes the collective welfare and harmonious coexistence of all species within the empire over individual gains. The T'au are relatively new to the galactic stage but have made significant strides in technology and society at an impressive rate. Unlike many other races, the T'au often resort to diplomacy and strategic alliances to expand their influence, integrating other species into the empire as willing participants. They offer protection and a place in their society to those who accept their ideals and contribute to the Greater Good, creating a diverse yet cohesive civilization.
Description: In addition to their own kind, the T'au Empire's military might is supplemented by auxiliary forces from allied species, such as the Kroot—ferocious warriors and trackers—and the Vespid, winged aliens with unique crystalline weaponry. These alliances showcase the T'au's commitment to unity and strength through diversity, as they carve out their place in a galaxy rife with conflict and war.

Genestealer, Tyranid Cult;
Background: Genestealer Cults are a blend of subterfuge and horror within the Warhammer 40k universe, representing the hidden infestation of the Tyranid Hive Fleets within unsuspecting societies. These cults originate from a single Genestealer, a Tyranid organism with the ability to infect and manipulate other species. The initial Genestealer infects a host, which leads to the creation of hybrid progeny—part Tyranid, part native species. These hybrids interbreed and spread across several generations, increasingly insinuating themselves into all levels of the host society. As the cult grows, the Genestealer's influence deepens, resulting in a secretive organization that operates from the shadows. Their ultimate goal is to prepare the planet for the arrival of their Tyranid kin, ensuring a weakened resistance when the Hive Fleet eventually invades. The cult's membership is diverse, ranging from almost fully human-like hybrids, which can easily blend into society, to more grotesque forms that bear a closer resemblance to their Tyranid lineage. These differences in appearance also denote a hierarchy within the cult, with each hybrid serving a specific role in the cult's agenda. At the pinnacle of this hierarchy is the Patriarch, the original Genestealer that founded the cult. This creature is both the psychic and spiritual center of the cult, exerting control over its members through a potent psychic connection. The cultists are fanatically loyal to the Patriarch, driven by a combination of psychic domination and religious fervor. 
Description: Genestealer Cults are adept at infiltrating key positions in society, often reaching into the military and political spheres. This allows them to destabilize a world's defenses and infrastructure, spreading dissent and chaos to make the planet ripe for conquest. When a Hive Fleet finally arrives, the cult rises up in rebellion, creating a fifth column that collaborates with the Tyranid invaders to ensure the planet's downfall. This process makes Genestealer Cults one of the most feared and insidious threats faced by civilizations across the galaxy.

Space Marines, Adeptus Astartes;
Background: Space Marines, also known as the Adeptus Astartes, are among the most elite and feared military forces in the Warhammer 40k universe. Created by the Emperor of Mankind during the Great Crusade, these superhuman warriors were designed to be the ultimate soldiers, tasked with the unification of humanity and the defense of the Imperium of Man against all threats, both alien and heretical. To create a Space Marine, a normal human—usually a youth—is subjected to intense genetic modification, involving the implantation of gene-seed organs derived from the DNA of the Emperor's own genetically-engineered sons, the Primarchs. This process endows them with extraordinary physical and mental abilities, including enhanced strength, toughness, and an unnaturally long lifespan.
Description: Space Marines are organized into Chapters, with each Chapter being a self-sufficient army complete with its own fleet, support personnel, and non-combatant serfs. A typical Chapter consists of approximately a thousand battle-brothers, ensuring they are flexible enough to respond quickly to threats across the vastness of space, yet powerful enough to face almost any enemy. Each Chapter is fiercely independent, with its own culture, colors, and battle tactics, often tracing its origins back to one of the original Legions that fought during the Horus Heresy—an intergalactic civil war that nearly tore the Imperium apart. Space Marines are the shield and sword of the Emperor, embodying the might of the Imperium's military. With their unshakeable loyalty and peerless combat skills, they stand as humanity's bulwark against the darkness that threatens to engulf the galaxy.

Imperial Guard, Guardsmen;
Background: The Imperial Guard, also known as the Astra Militarum, is the primary fighting force of the Imperium of Man in the Warhammer 40k universe, an immense organization comprising countless billions of soldiers drawn from a million worlds. Unlike the superhuman Space Marines, the Guard consists of regular human beings, often pressed into service through conscription. Their strength lies in their vast numbers, the ability to deploy overwhelming firepower, and the indomitable spirit of men and women fighting for the survival of humanity. The true might of the Imperial Guard lies not in the exceptional abilities of individual soldiers, but in their collective capacity to face unimaginable horrors. Through a combination of relentless barrages, waves of human courage, and the steely command of their officers, the Guard stands as a bulwark against the ceaseless darkness, holding the line in the name of the Emperor of Mankind.
Description: The typical armament of an Imperial Guardsman is the lasgun, a reliable and mass-produced energy weapon, along with flak armor for basic protection. Though individually they may seem insignificant compared to the immense threats of the galaxy, together they form a vast sea of humanity capable of holding back the tide of aliens, heretics, and daemons that seek to destroy the Imperium. The Imperial Guard employs a diverse array of military vehicles, ranging from the Leman Russ Battle Tank—a mainstay of their armored divisions—to colossal super-heavy tanks and artillery capable of laying waste to enemy fortifications and formations. This mechanized might is crucial, as it provides the firepower and mobility needed to support the human waves of the Astra Militarum. Leadership within the Guard is a mix of military hierarchy and political oversight. Commissars, the feared and respected political officers, are attached to Guard units to ensure loyalty and morale. They are notorious for their uncompromising discipline, often executing soldiers who show cowardice or insubordination to maintain the fighting spirit of their troops.

Adeptus Mechanicus, Techpriests;
Background: The Adeptus Mechanicus is a powerful and esoteric institution within the Warhammer 40k universe, charged with the maintenance of the Imperium of Man's technology and the production of its war machines. These Tech-Priests, as they are known, are not just mere mechanics; they are deeply religious figures who worship the Machine God, known as the Omnissiah, whom they often equate with the Emperor of Mankind. They believe in the Quest for Knowledge, seeking to recover and protect the technology from Mankind's golden past. Operating from their Forge Worlds—entire planets turned into gargantuan factories—the Adeptus Mechanicus holds a monopoly over the knowledge of manufacturing and technology. These worlds are the heart of the Imperium's military-industrial complex, churning out everything from simple firearms to the mightiest warships. Due to the sacred nature of their work, the Tech-Priests view machines as embodiments of the divine, and any innovation or invention is seen as an act of worship.
Description: The Adeptus Mechanicus also fields their own military forces, known as the Skitarii, or Tech-Guard. These soldiers are often enhanced with numerous cybernetic augmentations, making them more machine than man, and perfectly suited to the harsh environments and demands of the battlefield. Accompanying them are the battle-automata: robotic warriors programmed for destruction, and the awe-inspiring Titans, towering engines of war that can annihilate entire armies on their own. As the guardians of technology, the Adeptus Mechanicus is essential to the survival of the Imperium. They are the only ones capable of maintaining the arcane and often poorly understood machinery upon which the Imperium relies. Their understanding of technology is wrapped in ritual and mysticism, and their actions are often as unpredictable as they are secretive. Nonetheless, their role in the Imperium is unquestioned, for without the Adeptus Mechanicus, the Imperium would surely crumble.

Adepta Sororitas, Sisters of battle;
Background: The Sisters of Battle, formally known as the Adepta Sororitas, are the militant arm of the Ecclesiarchy—the official state church of the Imperium—and are amongst the most devoted and fearsome warriors in the Warhammer 40k universe. Composed entirely of women, these warriors are renowned for their unwavering faith in the God-Emperor of Mankind, a faith so strong that it provides them with a near-immunity to psychic manipulation and the temptations of Chaos.
Description: The Orders Militant are the most recognizable branch of the Adepta Sororitas, often seen on the front lines of the galaxy's most brutal conflicts. They don power armor similar to that of Space Marines, and are equipped with an arsenal that includes bolters, flamers, and melta weapons—tools that not only bring destruction but also symbolize the purifying aspects of their faith. The Sisters of Battle also make use of sacred relics and artifacts that are believed to carry the blessings of the Emperor himself. On the battlefield, the Sisters' acts of faith are more than mere legend; they are tangible manifestations of their devotion, capable of inspiring allies and striking fear into the hearts of their enemies. These acts can manifest in various miraculous ways, from healing the wounded to smiting the foe with divine fury. The Sisters of Battle are disciplined and formidable combatants, often leading the charge where the fighting is thickest, and rallying their troops with chants and prayers to the Emperor. Their organization is divided into various orders, each with its specialties and traditions, but all united in the cause of protecting the Imperium and its faith. The Adepta Sororitas not only serve as soldiers but also as a symbol of the Ecclesiarchy's power and the uncompromising nature of the Imperial Creed. With bolter and flame, they purge the unclean and stand as a bulwark against heresy, ensuring that the light of the Emperor endures amidst the darkness of the far future.
